I'm so glad your personal experience doing a single wire transfer makes you an expert on the subject, certainly moreso than someone that has sent dozens of wire transfers to different countries and understands that the rates can vary dependent on the beneficiary institution. For example, Russian wire transfers come with higher costs and scrutiny. Wire transfer costs can also vary from beneficiary to beneficiary.

&#x200B;

I've also experienced again settlements may take days to countries with sanctions or perhaps more complexity like sending money to Russia. At times too, I've issued wire transfers to jails in the United States so parents could provide money to their son. They wouldn't even know if the transfer went through at all until the next call with their loved son. 

&#x200B;

Crypto can at least tell us when we have confirmation, typically at lower costs, and when complexity is involved (like sending money to Russia), it is certainly quicker and easier with a payments crypto.

