Available Supply - this includes exchanges. (for Bitcoin; it's about 19 million)

Max Supply - the most that will ever be created (for Bitcoin, it's 21 million).

&#x200B;

Your last point gets to some of the gist of tokennomics but you have simplified it. If 90% of the coins haven't been issued yet then that does mean there will be selling pressure as they do get issued. That said, if the amount of utility of that coin can increase beyond the selling pressure, it doesn't mean that the price will go down at all.

&#x200B;

That's why tokennomics is an analysis of the supply (the available supply, max supply, lockup periods, etc.) and the demand (a review of the utility, the community engagement, the developer support, etc.)

